00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar human certified omics data to  power drug discovery this webinar is  part of the fifth annual drug discovery  and development  virtual conference and i am susie valdez  of lab roots and i will be your  moderator for today's event today's  educational web seminar is presented by  lab roots and sponsored by kyogen for  more information about our sponsor  please visit them at www.kyogen.com  now before we begin i want to remind  everyone that this event is interactive  we encourage you to first participate by  communicating with other attendees using  our new live  chat feature during the presentation you  can find that live chat located at the  right of your screen you can also  participate by submitting as many  questions as you want during the  presentation to do so simply type them  into the ask a question box and click  send we'll answer as many questions as  we have time for at the end of the  presentation  and if you have any trouble seeing or  hearing this presentation just click on  the help desk button located at the  bottom of your screen within the  navigation bar or from the lobby and  finally as a reminder this presentation  is educational and offers free  continuing education credits just click  on the continuing education credits link  located in the abstract window below the  presentation window and follow the  process to obtain those credits i now  present today's speaker dr joseph  pearson a global product manager at  kyogen digital insights for complete  biography of our speakers please visit  the biography tab at the left of your  screen dr pearson welcome you may now  begin your presence  my name is joseph pearson i am the  global product manager for omicsoft part  of kyogen digital insights  i will be talking today about why high  quality human certified data is really  essential for bioinformatics and data  science and drug discovery  before i begin i'll point out that the  kyogen products i mentioned are intended  for molecular biology applications  not for the diagnosis prevention  or treatment of a disease  now whether you are detecting subtle  signatures for disease  tissue  cell type or treatment response  or identifying new candidate indications  that are relevant to your target pathway  you need data that are precisely  relevant to the question you are asking  maybe you just need a strong reference  data set of normal and disease ohmic  signatures to compare against your  internal data  with rapidly growing public data  available for many diseases a common  approach is to utilize these resources  integrating different data sets to try  to generate a more robust signal  these data sets come from large  consortium projects like  tcga gtx  human cell landscape as well as from  thousands of data sets related to  individual published works to be found  in array express geo and sra  you also may have internal data sets  that can shed light  a key problem is that these data sets  must be discovered  unified  and centralized  to harness the value of the larger data  set for meta analysis  which uses  significant time  money and resources  without extensive unification and  centralization these data cannot be  easily discovered and explored by  biologists  bioinformaticians and data scientists  and you frequently end up with just a  small amount of usable data  and sometimes just a pile of useless  excel files  the goal of data science is to reveal  subtle patterns that aren't apparent by  looking variable by variable gene by  gene etc  the problem is  that it takes a tremendous amount of  time to whip data into the right shape  to perform real data science  in order to deeply analyze individual  data sets or  to analyze across multiple related data  sets to reveal consistent patterns you  need clean data  first  you need to find the data download the  data process and normalize the omix  experiments and then you need to  retrieve the metadata files that  describe each sample which may be in one  or multiple files and match these to the  omix experiments  you also need to  make sure that you understand the  meaning of each metadata field  clear definition of the metadata is even  more critical if you are integrating  multiple data sets because bad  unification  of data sets with mismatched metadata  fields will lead to bad interpretations  only after you spent way too much time  cleaning and organizing  the data from individual data sets then  unifying across the data sets can you  get to the interesting problems  looking for the patterns in the data  refining your algorithms to reveal the  patterns and testing the value of the  patterns with training sets and so forth  now there's a second related problem  clean metadata are essential to  capturing the biological relevance but  bioinformaticians and data scientists  are often focused on the task of  cleaning the omics data themselves  and they don't have the biological  perspective to capture the crucial  metadata so the individual data sets may  be properly analyzed but as far as  integrative analysis you frequently end  up with folders full of excel files that  really aren't much use  now what if data scientists could skip  over the time consuming data retrieval  and curation tasks and spend more time  on data science  with access to highly clean  consistent data  data scientists can skip over the time  consuming tasks of cleaning data  and avoid the pitfalls of poorly  cleaning metadata and get right to the  task of hunting for patterns across the  data  these same data can be used as a  repository to pull individual data sets  discoverable by biomarker signatures or  by metadata fields with clean  definitions and ontologies  whether looking for patterns across data  sets or hunting for the specific data  set that will answer your question  the biologists that you work with get  their answers quickly so they can design  the next set of experiments  given the huge amount of data available  it's tempting to aggregate all the data  through a huge automated pipeline omix  data can be run through an automated  pipeline or the pre-analyzed data can be  normalized after the fact  metadata can be integrated and  harmonization of metadata can be  achieved by automatically applying  ontologies or  natural language processing  this approach isn't bad when integrating  a couple of large consortium data sets  and if you are only interested in the  main sample metadata fields like tissue  and disease  because chances are good that a common  and recognizable ontology is being used  and the cleaning is simple enough  but  as you increase the number of data sets  to integrate or try to integrate more  data types or unify and interpret more  metadata fields like clinical parameters  treatment history  the applicability of automated methods  will quickly run out  the alternative to relying on automated  data integration is data that had been  curated and reviewed by scientists  humans with specific domain expertise  the ability to make judgments on  ambiguous data and iterate on the  curation processes with a plan to future  uses  this results in higher quality data with  lower error rates so downstream analysis  goes more smoothly  greater flexibility to integrate newer  data types and data structures  and greater flexibility in capturing and  structuring relevant metadata such as  from images and from full text  now the curation protocols can also be  designed with fair principles in mind  helping to make the data findable  accessible  interoperable and reusable  these are the goals  that we've had in mind over  our 25 years experience a quarter of a  century curating data and biomolecular  knowledge with curation now performed by  over 200 curation experts as part of  kaijan digital insights  to ensure highest quality kaijen has  developed detailed protocols for  curation of biomedical relationships  multi-omics data  and gene variant data  to consistently capture  the relevant information  causal biomedical relationships are  curated from biological findings from  publications such as molecule to  molecule as well as molecule to disease  function and localization  molecular pathways  and findings about regulators and  targets drugs and disease biomarkers  sample level ohmics data from disease  relevant  consortia and thousands of published  projects are carefully curated with  extensive controlled vocabularies and  curation rules apply across data sets to  maximize intercomparability  and the largest most up-to-date resource  for peer-reviewed gene variation data  linked to disease for both somatic and  hereditary mutations as well as  real-world data with observed variations  and we think that it's this dedication  to well-structured human-centered  curation that provides higher quality  starting points for data science  in fact if you would like to learn more  about some of the key lessons that we've  learned from our experience  we presented seven habits of good manual  curation  presented with the pistol alliance  and you can find this presentation  online at the postoya alliance website  and you can also find these seven habits  on the kaijan digital insights youtube  channel just search for kayajin manual  curation  for the remainder of this webinar i'm  going to focus on the kaijan digital  insights curated omix datasets which are  found in our omicsoft land databases  to date  cayegian curators has spent over 60  thousand hours of manual curation effort  to clean up thousands of  disease-relevant data sets found in  repositories like geo and array express  and large consortia like tcga gtx and  ccle  resulting in databases with over 650 000  samples ready for data science  applications  over the many years of experience  curating data sets and working with our  customers to define and refine curation  rules  we have established curation standards  to enable consistency within and across  data sets  making the data ready for revealing  deeper insights  our bioinformaticians  use consistent data analysis pipelines  to process the input data to make the  data sets more consistent and  cross-comparable  analyzed on standardized genomes and  gene models  and our statisticians use the  extensively curated sample metadata to  carefully model statistical comparisons  for studies to reveal the differential  gene expression signatures  and these data are available  as full flat file exports of the entire  databases which can be used by data  scientists for large-scale analyses and  these same data can be interactively  explored through our software sliced and  diced by bioinformaticians to find the  most relevant data sets for exploratory  work and prioritizing candidate  biomarkers  and these data are really great for  quickly answering biologist questions  like  is my gene expressed in a specific  tissue disease  cell type is it up or down regulated  after  treatment or is it higher in treatment  resistant patients  these questions can be answered using  our data quickly and confidently because  of the detailed manual curation and the  size of the curated databases  our curated omix data sets continue to  grow every quarter with data sets  representing hundreds of diseases in  oncology  and non-oncology disease  as well as expression in normal tissue  and cell lines and new single cell  transcriptomic data with the same depth  of manual curation as with our bulk  lands  so how do we know  that our depth of curation our extensive  curation and formatting rules and  ontologies is improving the value of the  data  firstly  our curators need to hunt down the  relevant metadata that are scattered  across data submissions supplementary  data and tables in the publication  and they also frequently see that the  data as they are submitted have mistakes  that cannot always be caught by  automated processes  in our experience  about 35  of data sets that our curators review  include typos in the metadata describing  the cell lines and treatments and so  forth  but about five percent of the time the  data are just not interpretable without  getting feedback from the authors  because during the review  we discover inconsistent terms  mislabeled treatments infections and so  forth  inconsistent sample groupings between  the data submission and the publication  and errors in the subject id to sample  id mapping  and so we are routinely contacting the  authors to clarify these inconsistencies  to make sure that we have the highest  quality data possible  so as one recent example  a project that  our curators were working on was  focusing on clinically significant  genetic differences between subtypes of  bcp acute lymphoblastic leukemia  thank you again dr pearson for your time  today and for your important research  and before we go i want to thank our  audience for joining us for today and  i'd also like to thank our sponsor  kaijin for sponsoring today's webinar  questions submitted today and during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  our speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  for two years until february 2024.  labyrinths will alert you via email when  it's available for a replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time take care  everyone

